  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAn upper level ridge axis will start it's approach today as it\nbegins to deamplify. After some valley fog this morning, we \nshould see plenty of sunshine the rest of today and a little \nbit of a breeze in the afternoon. Temperatures will be a touch \nwarmer today than yesterday but still right around (or just \nslightly above) normal for this time of the year, ranging from \nthe upper 60s and lower 70s north, to the upper 70s and lower \n80s south. Lows tonight will mainly be in the 50s as the ridge\naxis crosses the forecast area from west to east. By Tuesday\nmorning, we transition back into southwest flow aloft as the\nnext longwave trough approaches. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA slow moving deep trough digs into the region as a 990 mb surface \nlow swings through Quebec. The low-level response across the \nNortheast will be southerly flow increasing, which should really \nadvect in a juicy airmass as PWATs surge to 1.5-2.0\" by Tuesday \nnight; 90th percentile of climatology. There is a signal for some \nIVT transport with a tropical connection to the Gulf Coast. Combined \nwith freezing levels up around 13-14 kft, it looks to be an \nenvironment primed for efficient and locally heavy rainfall.\n\nCan begin to parse out potential convective evolution as hi-res \nguidance is just coming into range this evening. Currently strong \nmodel consensus we will remain north of the warm sector on Tuesday, \nwith a late arrival of storms during the evening hours, past peak \nheating. Respectable MUCAPE suggests thunderstorms may persist into \nthe night locally, but with high freezing levels and low-level \nstability, not concerned about severe weather. SPC Day 3 outlook \nclips SW NH with a marginal risk, where a slightly earlier storm \narrival is possible.\n\nAfter the initial lead shortwave pushes offshore early Wednesday, \ncurrent indications are the region sees some modest\ndestabilization. Also some signals for a bit of a \"cold pool \naloft\" type of setup as the upper-level trough swings in, with \ncooling temperatures aloft. An inspection of regional soundings \nshows weak low-level shear, but strong deep-layer shear. Most \nlikely concern would be for gusty winds and small hail, in \naddition to a second straight day of locally heavy rainfall.\n\nShould generally have near seasonable temperatures this upcoming \nweek. Strong cold front Wednesday night should really dry things out \nwith dew points in the 40s-50s for the remainder of the week.",
